Indeed.  I'm having considerable success in convincing people to use a regular 
past tense of "u".  Past tense of "think" is "thunk", of "click" is "cluck" 
and of "print" is "prunt".  I've now heard second generation usage ...

On a more serious note, the interest in the original is the accuracy of the 
quantitative predictions.


